WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.255 --> 00:00:05.038
An order went through with zero items.
The ticket says the smallest valid

2
00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:05.881
order is one.

3
00:00:06.361 --> 00:00:09.036
Open the training checkout in Visual
Studio Code,

4
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:12.102
with its terminal connected to the
approved workspace.

5
00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:17.238
Run the command on screen from the
directory containing the validator and

6
00:00:17.238 --> 00:00:17.955
its tests.

7
00:00:18.616 --> 00:00:23.213
Keep that command handy. We will use
exactly the same one twice more.

8
00:00:23.884 --> 00:00:27.985
Three tests pass. Read their names
with me. They try five, one,

9
00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:29.529
and negative one.

10
00:00:30.341 --> 00:00:33.237
Which test would catch an order for
zero items?

11
00:00:35.797 --> 00:00:39.938
Try the missing value directly. Import
the same public function that the

12
00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:40.746
tests use.

13
00:00:41.187 --> 00:00:45.589
Call it with zero and print the return
value. There it is. True.

14
00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:50.451
Read the ticket beside that result. An
order with zero items must be

15
00:00:50.451 --> 00:00:52.620
rejected, so we expected false.

16
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.594
The existing tests can all pass while
this particular call gives

17
00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:58.735
the wrong answer.

18
00:00:59.406 --> 00:01:01.901
Leave the source as it is for a
moment.

19
00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:06.567
This is our chance to find out whether
a new test can actually

20
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:07.755
detect the defect.

21
00:01:08.922 --> 00:01:12.243
Here is a question you can put to
Copilot in Plan mode.

22
00:01:12.844 --> 00:01:17.620
Which expression admits zero, and what
will happen if we assert that zero

23
00:01:17.771 --> 00:01:18.802
must be rejected?

24
00:01:19.992 --> 00:01:22.687
Include the issue, the validator, and
its tests.

25
00:01:23.397 --> 00:01:27.463
Check the prediction yourself on line
three. Substitute zero for quantity.

26
00:01:28.045 --> 00:01:30.250
Is zero greater than or equal to zero?

27
00:01:31.482 --> 00:01:34.646
Yes. The equality part is enough to
let it through.

28
00:01:36.578 --> 00:01:41.287
Now try one and negative one in your
head. One is greater than zero.

29
00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:45.487
Negative one is smaller. The equality
at the boundary explains

30
00:01:45.578 --> 00:01:46.610
all three outcomes.

31
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:52.570
Give Agent a small first job. Add one
zero regression to the existing test

32
00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.977
class and leave the validator
untouched.

33
00:01:55.577 --> 00:01:57.799
You can also type the two lines shown
here.

34
00:01:58.591 --> 00:02:02.680
Read the assertion from the inside
out. The function receives zero.

35
00:02:03.161 --> 00:02:05.875
The assertion requires its result to
be false.

36
00:02:06.746 --> 00:02:12.030
A test that simply checks whether zero
is false would tell us nothing about

37
00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:12.872
our validator.

38
00:02:13.603 --> 00:02:16.402
Before running it, inspect the changed
file list.

39
00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:18.810
You should see the test file alone.

40
00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:23.051
That lets us attribute the next
failure to the behavior already

41
00:02:23.082 --> 00:02:24.636
present in the starting code.

42
00:02:26.084 --> 00:02:28.945
Run the same command again. First
check the total.

43
00:02:29.337 --> 00:02:30.913
Four tests ran this time.

44
00:02:31.594 --> 00:02:35.766
Now find the failure and follow it
back to the line that calls the

45
00:02:35.766 --> 00:02:37.187
validator with zero.

46
00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:40.834
Read the assertion message. True is
not false.

47
00:02:41.225 --> 00:02:43.300
That is the observation we predicted.

48
00:02:43.881 --> 00:02:47.342
The three earlier cases still pass.
Keep this output with

49
00:02:47.342 --> 00:02:48.492
the test-only patch.

50
00:02:49.464 --> 00:02:52.739
Suppose the runner had stopped at an
import error.

51
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.802
What would you know about zero?

52
00:02:56.273 --> 00:03:00.554
Very little. Fix the setup and rerun
until the assertion itself executes.

53
00:03:01.735 --> 00:03:05.181
Now the regression can catch the bug.
Open the production diff.

54
00:03:05.693 --> 00:03:06.888
Remove the equals sign.

55
00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:10.251
The expression now asks whether
quantity is strictly

56
00:03:10.251 --> 00:03:11.268
greater than zero.

57
00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:15.870
Keep the public function signature and
the new assertion as they are.

58
00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.455
Make a prediction before the next run.
Zero is equal to zero,

59
00:03:20.845 --> 00:03:22.998
so the new expression returns false.

60
00:03:23.698 --> 00:03:28.319
Five and one should still pass.
Negative one should still be rejected.

61
00:03:30.378 --> 00:03:34.616
Run that saved command a third time.
The zero test now passes,

62
00:03:35.027 --> 00:03:36.580
and the total is still four.

63
00:03:37.381 --> 00:03:40.261
Compare the failure and the pass side
by side.

64
00:03:40.592 --> 00:03:43.471
The method name and its assertion are
identical.

65
00:03:44.302 --> 00:03:47.154
For a handoff, give your teammate the
starting checkout,

66
00:03:47.485 --> 00:03:51.938
the test-only diff and its failing
output, then the source diff and its

67
00:03:51.948 --> 00:03:52.959
passing output.

68
00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.841
They can apply the changes in that
order and repeat your result.

69
00:03:59.027 --> 00:04:01.141
Try the same method on a separate
exercise.

70
00:04:01.552 --> 00:04:06.210
An order may contain one through one
hundred items, including one hundred.

71
00:04:06.891 --> 00:04:10.419
The draft on screen uses a strict
comparison at each end.

72
00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:15.163
Pause the video here. Write the
expected answers for ninety-nine,

73
00:04:15.474 --> 00:04:17.785
one hundred, and one hundred and one.

74
00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:21.752
Which single regression would expose
the mistake before you

75
00:04:21.782 --> 00:04:22.716
edit the function?

76
00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:28.345
Ready?

77
00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:34.818
The answers are true, true, and false.
The draft rejects exactly one of those

78
00:04:34.818 --> 00:04:36.708
valid examples, one hundred.

79
00:04:37.559 --> 00:04:41.001
Write an assertion that accepts one
hundred. Watch it fail,

80
00:04:41.392 --> 00:04:44.433
then include equality at the upper
limit and rerun.

81
00:04:45.224 --> 00:04:48.132
Notice how the ticket decided which
way equality belongs.

82
00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:53.392
Reading the rule, testing the edge,
and replaying the same check will

83
00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:55.342
serve you at either end of a range.
